course: dictionary
lesson: 7694
title: Proven: shown to be true
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Proven is an adjective, and it often comes before a noun: a proven fact, a proven method. It means shown to be true by facts or evidence, or tested and known to work. Think of it as "someone checked it, and it held up."
explain: Proven is also the past form of the verb prove, which means to show that something is true. You will often see it after has or have: «It has been proven.» Proved and proven are both past forms, but proven is common in science and adverts.
explain: Read this sentence: «Superstitions like these are beliefs, not proven facts, but a polite guest follows the house rules.» Superstitions are beliefs that nobody has shown to be true. The word not is the clue: the writer says they lack evidence.
explain: Always ask what the evidence is. If an advert says a cream is "proven to work", look for the tests behind that claim. To check your meaning, swap in "shown to be true by evidence". If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Proven means shown to be true by facts or evidence.
fact: A proven method is a way of doing something that has been tested and is known to work.
fact: Proven is the past participle of the verb prove, as in «It has been proven.»
fact: «Not proven» is a court verdict in Scotland, meaning there was not enough evidence.
fact: In adverts, «proven» is used to make you trust a product, so ask what the evidence is.
exercise: «Superstitions like these are beliefs, not proven facts.» What does proven mean here? | *Shown to be true by facts or evidence | Very old and very famous | Written down in a book | Made up by a clever guest
exercise: A cream advert says it is «proven to work». What should you ask? | Who drew the picture on the label? | *What tests show that it works? | What colour is the box?
exercise: Which word means tested and known to work? | Gentle | Sleepy | Lost | *Proven
test: «The scientist said the rule was proven by many tests.» Proven here means | *Shown to be true by tests | Lost forever | Written in red ink | Guessed and never checked
test: Which form is the past participle of prove, used after has or have? | proves | prove | *proven | proud
exam: «Not proven» is a Scottish court verdict. What does it mean? | Everyone is innocent for sure | The judge has proven the case wrong | The case is closed for ever | *There was not enough evidence
